    "verdict": "artifact changes and rate changes are different questions: 3 of 5 substitutions change an artifact, but only 2 of 4 comparable rate comparisons separate (per-logical-bit scale; 1 separate on the any-observable scale)",
    "interpretation": "an artifact-level substitution is not automatically a rate-level substitution. The noise coordinate changes both, because it is baked into the circuit. The decoder substitution changes no artifact and no rate, because the decoder's task is unchanged. The extractor substitution is scale-dependent: it changes the circuit and moves the per-logical-bit rate (the two extraction schedules distribute the same physical errors differently across the 12 observables), while the any-observable failure rate is unchanged. A study that substitutes a decoder and reports 'no significant difference' is reporting a real property of the substitution, not a null result about the software."
